From f7f5dbcee470481aabbfadd190919782659466bc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bateske <kevin@bateske.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 21:09:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] CHGfx P5: gfx_blit4k remaps and inks through a table of pixel
 pairs

Remap and ink fold into one colour map M (the remap, then the ink over
all but 15; M[15] is always 15, so the key stays the key and the
table's entry 15 is still never read), spread over a 256-byte table T
of pixel pairs that each remapped or inked call builds (64 word stores)
on the stack of a function of its own: a load per two pixels instead of
a nibble at a time, ~22 instructions a source word instead of ~80. A
mirrored remap is the same loop reading from the row's last byte back
(T holds the pairs swapped). Plain, mirrored and dithered blits build no
table and keep their stack depth.

All paths: the copy loop is picked once a row instead of every flag
being tested every word, the mirror is a rotate and two delta swaps
(fewer live constants, no spills), and the merge loop keeps the previous
word in a register and tests at the bottom (~26 instructions a word
stored, was ~28).

Output unchanged: the host test (P4's cases, plus every pixel pair with
both flips at every x phase, tables with entries above 15 and of 15, a
table changed between calls, REMAP without a table) passes against the
same pixel-at-a-time reference, and CHGfx's own 19,962 checks pass. SRAM
part 538 -> 632 bytes, still no calls out of it; remapped and inked
calls take 256 more bytes of stack. Expected on the board (32x32, from
the disassembly and a cycle model fitted to P4's measured numbers):
remapped ~235 us (was 455), mirrored ~195 (237), plain ~134 / ~153 at
x % 8 = 0 / 3 (149 / 170). Images that do not call it are byte-identical
(CHWords, CHWordWheel, CHCrossword against the pristine and the P4
CHGfx).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
